You can create a new parameter `P2(A,C)` in GAMS using the `execute_unload` and `execute_load` functions in conjunction with the `reindex` keyword. However, a more straightforward approach would be to use the implicit mapping of the parameter `P1` to create `P2`. 

Here's how you can achieve this:

```gams
Parameter P1(A,B) / ... /;
Parameter P2(A,C) like=P1[A,*];
```

In this code, `like=P1[A,*]` tells GAMS to create `P2` by copying the values of `P1` but with a new index `C` that is derived from `B` by the implicit mapping between `B` and `C` (which you mentioned is a one-to-one mapping). This approach assumes that the mapping between `B` and `C` is already defined in the data of `P1`.

However, if `P1` does not have a one-to-one mapping between `B` and `C` but you know the mapping, you can create `P2` using the following code:

```gams
Parameter P1(A,B) / ... /;
Parameter P2(A,C);
Set BtoC / ... /;  // Define the mapping from B to C
BtoC(B,C) = ...;  // Define the values of the mapping
P2(A,C) = P1[A,BtoC(B,C)];
```

In this code, `BtoC(B,C)` is a set that defines the mapping from `B` to `C`. The values of `P2` are then calculated by looking up the corresponding value in `P1` using the mapped value of `B` in `BtoC(B,C)`.

