**Save Current Page Plugin**

This is a simple Chrome plugin that saves the contents of the current page to a file.

### Directory Structure

```markdown
save-current-page/
|---manifest.json
|---popup.html
|---popup.js
|---save-page.js
|---icon.png
```

### manifest.json

```json
{
  "name": "Save Current Page",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Save the contents of the current page",
  "permissions": ["activeTab"],
  "browser_action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html"
  },
  "icons": {
    "16": "icon16.png",
    "48": "icon48.png",
    "128": "icon128.png"
  }
}
```

### popup.html

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Save Current Page</title>
    <style>
      body {
        width: 200px;
        height: 100px;
        font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
        text-align: center;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Save Current Page</h1>
    <button id="save-button">Save Page</button>
    <script src="popup.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

### popup.js

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
  const saveButton = document.getElementById("save-button");
  saveButton.addEventListener("click", savePage);
});

function savePage() {
  chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, function (tabs) {
    chrome.tabs.sendMessage(tabs[0].id, { action: "save-page" }, function (response) {
      alert("Page saved successfully!");
    });
  });
}
```

### save-page.js (content script)

```javascript
function savePage() {
  const pageContent = document.documentElement.outerHTML;
  const blob = new Blob([pageContent], { type: "text/html" });
  const link = document.createElement("a");
  link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
  link.download = "saved-page.html";
  link.click();
  URL.revokeObjectURL(link.href);
}

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(function (request, sender, sendResponse) {
  if (request.action === "save-page") {
    savePage();
  }
});
```

### Explanation

This plugin uses a content script (`save-page.js`) to get the HTML content of the current page and a popup script (`popup.js`) to handle the save button click event. When the save button is clicked, the popup script sends a message to the content script to save the page.

### Installing the Plugin

1. Create a new directory for the plugin and navigate to it in your terminal.
2. Create the files `manifest.json`, `popup.html`, `popup.js`, `save-page.js`, and `icon.png` in the directory.
3. Fill in the code for each file.
4. Go to the Chrome extensions page by typing `chrome://extensions/` in the address bar.
5. Enable developer mode by toggling the switch in the top-right corner.
6. Click "Load unpacked" and select the directory containing the plugin files.
7. The plugin should now be visible in the extensions list.

### Testing the Plugin

1. Go to any webpage and click the plugin's icon in the top-right corner.
2. Click the "Save Page" button in the popup that appears.
3. The plugin should save the current page to a file called `saved-page.html`.

