**Computing Fibonacci Number Modulo p**
=====================================================

We can use matrix exponentiation to efficiently compute the n-th Fibonacci number modulo p. This method has a time complexity of O(log n), making it much faster than the naive recursive approach for large values of n.

**JavaScript Function**
-----------------------

```javascript
function multiply(matrix, mod) {
    let result = [
        [1, 1],
        [1, 0]
    ];

    for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        for (let j = 0; j < 2; j++) {
            for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) {
                result[i][j] = (result[i][j] + matrix[i][k] * matrix[k][j]) % mod;
            }
        }
    }

    return result;
}

function matrixPower(matrix, n, mod) {
    if (n === 1) {
        return matrix;
    }

    if (n % 2 === 0) {
        let halfPow = matrixPower(matrix, n / 2, mod);
        return multiply(halfPow, halfPow, mod);
    } else {
        return multiply(matrix, matrixPower(matrix, n - 1, mod), mod);
    }
}

function fibonacci(n, p) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return n;
    }

    let matrix = [
        [1, 1],
        [1, 0]
    ];

    let resultMatrix = matrixPower(matrix, n - 1, p);

    return resultMatrix[0][0];
}

console.log(fibonacci(10, 100));  // Example usage
```

**Explanation**
--------------

The `multiply` function multiplies two 2x2 matrices modulo p.

The `matrixPower` function calculates the power of a 2x2 matrix. It uses the divide and conquer approach to reduce the time complexity.

The `fibonacci` function uses the `matrixPower` function to compute the n-th Fibonacci number modulo p.

This implementation has a time complexity of O(log n), making it much faster than the naive recursive approach for large values of n.

