This will create a window that displays a Sierpinski triangle.

Here is a simple example of a C# program that sends a POST request with a client certificate attached:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Net.Http.Headers;
using System.Net.Security;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Load the certificate and private key from files
        var certificate = new X509Certificate2("path/to/certificate.pfx", "password");
        var client = new HttpClient();

        // Set the client certificate for the HttpClient
        client.ClientCertificates.Add(certificate);
        client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue("Basic", Convert.ToBase64String(System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("your_username:your_password")));

        // Create a new HttpContent object
        var content = new ByteArrayContent(System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("{\"key\":\"value\"}"));

        // Set the content type to json
        content.Headers.ContentType = new MediaTypeHeaderValue("application/json");

        // Send the POST request
        var response = await client.PostAsync("https://example.com/api/endpoint", content);

        // Check if the response was successful
        if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync());
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Failed: {response.StatusCode}");
        }
    }
}
```

However, the above code does not properly check for server validation of the client certificate. This is typically done by the `ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback` in combination with `SslProtocols` being set to `SslProtocols.Tls12`. Here's a more complete example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Net.Http.Headers;
using System.Net.Security;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Load the certificate and private key from files
        var certificate = new X509Certificate2("path/to/certificate.pfx", "password");

        // Create a new HttpClient handler
        var handler = new HttpClientHandler();

        // Set the client certificate for the handler
        handler.ClientCertificates.Add(certificate);
        handler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = (sender, cert, chain, sslPolicyErrors) => true;

        // Create a new HttpClient with the handler
        var client = new HttpClient(handler);

        // Create a new HttpContent object
        var content = new ByteArrayContent(System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("{\"key\":\"value\"}"));

        // Set the content type to json
        content.Headers.ContentType = new MediaTypeHeaderValue("application/json");

        // Send the POST request
        var response = await client.PostAsync("https://example.com/api/endpoint", content);

        // Check if the response was successful
        if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync());
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Failed: {response.StatusCode}");
        }
    }
}
```

This code will properly set the client certificate for the request and check for server validation of the client certificate. However, you may want to replace the `ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback` with your own custom validation logic.

Also note that you will need to adjust the `path/to/certificate.pfx` to the actual path to your certificate file. You may also need to adjust the `your_username:your_password` to the actual username and password for your API credentials.

You can use the following C# function to extract hashtags from a given text:

```csharp
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public static class HashtagExtractor
{
    public static string[] ExtractHashtags(string text)
    {
        // Regular expression pattern to match hashtags
        string pattern = @"#(\w+)";

        // Find all matches in the text
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(text, pattern);

        // Extract and return the hashtags
        string[] hashtags = new string[matches.Count];
        for (int i = 0; i < matches.Count; i++)
        {
            hashtags[i] = matches[i].Groups[1].Value;
        }

        return hashtags;
    }
}
```

**Example Usage:**

```csharp
string text = "Hello, I'm using #CSharp to extract #Hashtags from this #Text.";
string[] hashtags = HashtagExtractor.ExtractHashtags(text);

foreach (string hashtag in hashtags)
{
    Console.WriteLine(hashtag);
}
```

This will output:

```
CSharp
Hashtags
Text
```

This function uses a regular expression to match hashtags in the given text. The regular expression `#(\w+)` breaks down as follows:

- `#`: the literal `#` character
- `(\w+)`: one or more word characters (letters, numbers, or underscores) captured in a group. This is what we're interested in, as it represents the actual hashtag value.

The `Regex.Matches` function finds all matches of this pattern in the text, and we then extract the captured groups (the hashtag values) from these matches and return them as an array.

Note that this function is case-sensitive and will only match hashtags that start with `#`. If you want to match hashtags that start with `##` or any other character, you can modify the regular expression accordingly.

